# Section II. Define Phase (20 of 100 scored; 10 per Tigertest test)
CAT = "cssgbDefine"; SEG = "DEF"
Q = [
("When is DMAIC a poor choice for a problem?",
 "The cause and fix are known", ["The root cause is unknown", "The process has data to study", "Defects cost a lot each year"],
 "DMAIC suits problems with an unknown cause and measurable data. If the solution is obvious, a just-do-it fix or a short kaizen is faster."),
("A steering team ranks candidate projects. Which factor matters most in selecting one?",
 "Business benefit against effort", ["Which department asked first", "How many people it involves", "How new the analysis tools are"],
 "Project selection weighs expected savings, customer impact and strategic fit against cost, time and risk, often in a prioritization matrix."),
("A purchase-to-pay process passes through purchasing, receiving and accounts payable. What challenge does this create?",
 "No one function owns the flow", ["The process has no customer", "Every step adds customer value", "Data cannot be collected"],
 "Processes that cross departments suffer hand-off delays and finger-pointing because each function optimizes its own part. A process owner for the whole flow helps."),
("Which item marks a process boundary in a project's scope?",
 "Its first and last steps", ["The project budget", "The team's meeting time", "The sponsor's job title and level"],
 "Process boundaries are the start and end points that define what the project will and will not change. SIPOC is a common way to set them."),
("A hospital compares its patient discharge process with a hotel's checkout process. What type of benchmarking is this?",
 "Best practice", ["Competitive", "Internal process", "Collaborative"],
 "Best practice benchmarking looks outside the industry at an organization that excels at a similar function. Competitive benchmarking compares with direct rivals."),
("Several hospitals in a network agree to share their infection data to learn from one another. Which benchmarking type is this?",
 "Collaborative", ["Competitive", "Best practice", "Reverse engineering"],
 "In collaborative benchmarking organizations agree to share data and practices openly for mutual benefit, often through a consortium or association."),
("A project will change how the warehouse picks orders. Who is an internal customer of the project?",
 "The packing team downstream", ["The end buyer of the product", "The shipping carrier", "A government regulator"],
 "Internal customers are people inside the organization who receive the output of a process step. The end buyer is an external customer."),
("A SIPOC places which element immediately before the process steps?",
 "Inputs", ["Outputs", "Customers", "Controls"],
 "SIPOC lists suppliers, inputs, process, outputs and customers in that order. Inputs feed the high-level process steps; outputs leave them."),
("A team wants rich, detailed opinions from eight key customers on why they cancelled. Which method fits best?",
 "One-to-one interviews", ["A mass email survey", "A warranty data review", "A check sheet"],
 "Interviews allow probing follow-up questions with a small number of people. Large surveys give breadth but little depth on reasons."),
("A survey asks: \"How satisfied are you with our fast and friendly service?\" What is wrong with it?",
 "It asks two things at once", ["It uses a rating scale", "It is too short to be useful", "It names the service"],
 "This is a double-barreled question: a customer may find service fast but not friendly. Each question should cover one idea, worded without bias."),
("Customers say \"the app is hard to use.\" Which tool turns that statement into measurable requirements?",
 "A CTQ tree", ["A Gantt chart", "A control chart", "A spaghetti diagram"],
 "A critical to quality tree breaks a broad need into drivers and then into specific, measurable requirements, such as tasks completed in three taps or fewer."),
("In the Kano model, what happens when a basic (must-be) requirement is fully met?",
 "Satisfaction stays neutral", ["Customers are delighted", "Satisfaction keeps rising", "Customers start to complain"],
 "Must-be features are expected. Missing them causes strong dissatisfaction, but delivering them only avoids complaints. Delighters cause delight when present."),
("A feature customers did not ask for delights them when added, and its absence causes no complaint. Which Kano category is it?",
 "Attractive", ["Must-be", "One-dimensional", "Indifferent"],
 "Attractive features raise satisfaction when present but cause no dissatisfaction when absent. Over time they tend to drift into must-be requirements."),
("In quality function deployment, what does the house of quality relate?",
 "Needs to technical measures", ["Costs to selling prices", "Team roles to project tasks", "Suppliers to process inputs"],
 "The house of quality is a matrix that links each voice of the customer need to the design or technical characteristics that satisfy it, weighted by importance."),
("Which is a key difference between agile and traditional top-down (waterfall) project management?",
 "Agile works in short cycles", ["Agile needs no customer input", "Waterfall changes scope often", "Waterfall has no plan"],
 "Agile breaks work into short cycles with frequent feedback and reprioritization. Top-down methods plan the full scope in advance and move through phases in sequence."),
("Which element belongs in a project charter's problem statement?",
 "Baseline data on the gap", ["The root cause of the problem", "The chosen solution", "The team's training plan"],
 "A problem statement describes what is wrong, where, when and how big, with baseline data. It should not assume a cause or a solution."),
("A project's scope keeps growing as stakeholders add requests. What is this called?",
 "Scope creep", ["Scope freeze", "Gold standard", "Baseline drift"],
 "Scope creep is uncontrolled growth in a project's scope. A clear in-scope and out-of-scope list in the charter, and change control, help prevent it."),
("A project aims to cut order processing time. Which is the best consequential metric to watch?",
 "Order entry error rate", ["Order processing time", "Number of team members", "Project budget spent"],
 "A consequential metric tracks a side effect the improvement could cause. Rushing order processing could raise errors, so the error rate is watched alongside the primary metric."),
("Which management and planning tool draws project tasks as boxes joined by arrows to show their order, dependencies and durations?",
 "Activity network diagram", ["Affinity diagram", "Tree diagram", "Matrix diagram"],
 "An activity network diagram (arrow diagram) sequences tasks and their durations, which lets the team find the critical path and the slack on other tasks."),
("A PERT estimate uses optimistic 3 days, most likely 9 days and pessimistic 21 days. What is the expected duration?",
 "10 days", ["9 days", "11 days", "18 days"],
 "PERT expected time is (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (3 + 36 + 21) / 6 = 60 / 6 = 10 days. A simple average of the three gives 11."),
("What is the purpose of a toll-gate review?",
 "To approve the next phase", ["To assign blame for delays", "To audit the finance team", "To train new Green Belts"],
 "At each DMAIC phase gate the sponsor or champion reviews deliverables and decides whether the project goes on, changes course or stops."),
("A team breaks the goal \"reduce billing errors\" into sub-goals and then into specific actions, level by level. Which planning tool is this?",
 "Tree diagram", ["Affinity diagram", "Interrelationship digraph", "Activity network diagram"],
 "A tree diagram breaks a broad goal into successively finer levels of detail until the branches are specific actions or measures. A CTQ tree is one use of it."),
("A Green Belt needs to summarize a project's progress on one page for a phase review. Which tool fits best?",
 "A storyboard", ["A raw data file", "A fishbone diagram only", "An email thread"],
 "A storyboard summarizes the problem, data, analysis and actions phase by phase, so managers can follow the project's logic at a glance."),
("Which question is part of a project's risk analysis?",
 "How likely is each risk?", ["Who will present the results?", "Which room will the team use?", "What font is the report in?"],
 "Project risk analysis rates each risk's likelihood and impact on schedule, cost, resources and stakeholders, then plans responses, sometimes with an RPN."),
("At project closure, against what are the project's results judged?",
 "The goals in the charter", ["The hours the team worked", "Other projects' savings", "The original budget alone"],
 "The closure review compares results with the charter's goals and metrics, confirms the sponsor and process owner accept them, and stores the documentation."),
("In an affinity diagram, once sticky notes are sorted into groups, what is added to each group?",
 "A header card naming the theme", ["The name of each idea's author", "A risk priority number", "A control limit"],
 "Members sort the notes silently into natural groups, then write a header card that captures each group's theme. An interrelationship digraph can then link the groups."),
("In an interrelationship digraph, the idea with the most outgoing arrows is usually:",
 "A key driver", ["A key outcome", "An unrelated idea", "A data error"],
 "Arrows point from cause to effect. An item with many outgoing arrows drives many others, so it is a likely root cause to work on."),
("Which planning tool lists what could go wrong at each step of a plan and the countermeasures?",
 "Process decision program chart", ["Activity network diagram", "Affinity diagram", "Prioritization matrix"],
 "A PDPC branches from each task to possible problems and then to countermeasures, so contingencies are planned before they are needed."),
("A team weights criteria and scores options to choose one improvement. Which tool is this?",
 "Prioritization matrix", ["SWOT analysis", "Activity network diagram", "Interrelationship digraph"],
 "A prioritization matrix scores each option against weighted criteria and totals the scores, so the choice is made on agreed criteria rather than opinion."),
("A line produces 380 units with 57 defects found in total. What is the defects per unit (DPU)?",
 "0.15", ["0.13", "0.85", "6.67"],
 "DPU = total defects / units = 57 / 380 = 0.15. 380 / 57 gives 6.67, the inverted ratio."),
("A process has three steps with first-pass yields of 97%, 92% and 95%. What is the rolled throughput yield?",
 "About 84.8%", ["About 94.7%", "About 92.0%", "About 89.0%"],
 "RTY multiplies the step yields: 0.97 x 0.92 x 0.95 = 0.848. Averaging the yields gives 94.7%, which overstates the chance a unit passes every step first time."),
("A team inspects 460 forms, each with 6 opportunities for error, and finds 37 errors. What is the DPMO?",
 "About 13,406", ["About 80,435", "About 1,341", "About 2,234"],
 "DPMO = defects / (units x opportunities) x 1,000,000 = 37 / 2,760 x 1,000,000, about 13,406. Leaving out the 6 opportunities gives 80,435."),
("A process runs at 6,210 DPMO. Using the conventional 1.5 sigma shift, what is its sigma level?",
 "About 4.0", ["About 2.5", "About 3.0", "About 5.5"],
 "6,210 DPMO is the long-term defect rate of a 4 sigma process: the long-term z of about 2.5 plus the 1.5 shift gives 4.0."),
("Which cost is an internal failure cost of poor quality?",
 "Scrap and rework", ["Warranty claims", "Supplier audits", "Operator training"],
 "Internal failure costs arise from defects found before delivery, such as scrap and rework. Warranty claims are external failure; audits are appraisal; training is prevention."),
("A director emails a policy change to every department at once. Which communication direction is this?",
 "Top-down", ["Bottom-up", "Horizontal", "Lateral peer"],
 "Top-down communication flows from leaders to the workforce. Bottom-up flows from employees to managers, and horizontal flows between peers or departments."),
("A project has met its goals, and the team reviews its work, celebrates and disbands. Which team stage is this?",
 "Adjourning", ["Performing", "Norming", "Storming"],
 "Adjourning, the stage added after forming, storming, norming and performing, is when a team wraps up, recognizes its members and breaks up. Performing is the productive stage before it."),
("Team members accept a senior manager's view without discussion, though some privately disagree. What is this?",
 "Groupthink", ["Floundering", "Feuding", "Digression"],
 "Groupthink is the push for agreement that silences doubts. A facilitator can counter it by inviting dissent or using anonymous voting."),
("In a RACI chart, the person who has final ownership of a task and signs it off is:",
 "Accountable", ["Responsible", "Consulted", "Informed"],
 "Responsible people do the work; one accountable person owns the outcome and approves it; consulted people give input; informed people are kept up to date."),
("Who in a Six Sigma deployment usually removes barriers and secures resources for a project?",
 "The champion or sponsor", ["The process operator", "A team member from operations", "The external auditor"],
 "The champion, often the sponsor, is a manager who backs the project, clears organizational barriers and provides resources. The Black Belt leads the technical work."),
("A team uses nominal group technique. What happens after members write ideas silently?",
 "Each shares one idea in turn", ["The leader picks the best idea", "Ideas are debated freely", "Ideas are discarded"],
 "In NGT members generate ideas alone, share them round robin without debate, clarify them, then rank them independently, so quieter members are heard equally."),
]
